                    ┌─────────────────────┐
                    │     Game / App      │
                    │   Game Logic / ECS  │
                    └──────────┬──────────┘
                               │
                    ┌──────────▼──────────┐
                    │     Engine Core     │
                    ├─────────────────────┤
                    │ Game Loop           │
                    │ Time / Clock        │
                    │ Event System        │
                    │ Entity Management    │
                    │ Resource Management  │
                    └──────────┬──────────┘
                               │
        ┌──────────────────────┼──────────────────────┐
        │                      │                      │
┌───────▼───────┐      ┌───────▼───────┐      ┌──────▼───────┐
│   Rendering   │      │    Physics    │      │     Audio    │
├───────────────┤      ├───────────────┤      ├──────────────┤
│ Renderer      │      │ Collision     │      │ Sound        │
│ Materials     │      │ Rigid Bodies  │      │ Music        │
│ Shaders       │      │ Raycast       │      │ Mixer        │
│ Textures      │      │ Constraints   │      │ 3D Audio     │
│ Lighting      │      │ Simulation    │      │ Effects      │
└───────┬───────┘      └───────────────┘      └──────────────┘
        │
┌───────▼───────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    Platform Layer                     │
├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Window │ Input │ File System │ Threads │ Networking   │
└───────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┘
                        │
                ┌───────▼────────┐
                │     OS / GPU   │
                │ Windows/Linux  │
                │ macOS / Vulkan │
                │ OpenGL / DX12  │
                └────────────────┘

                ## 🎮 Game Engine Components

A game engine is usually divided into these major components:

```text
                    GAME ENGINE
                         │
        ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐
        │                │                │
       CORE           RUNTIME           TOOLS
        │                │                │
        ▼                ▼                ▼
   Game Loop         Rendering         Editor
   Memory            Physics           Profiler
   Events            Audio             Debugger
   ECS               Animation         Asset Tools
   Threads           Input
                     Networking
                     Scripting
```

### 1. Core Engine

The foundation of the engine.

* Game Loop
* Time / Delta Time
* Memory Management
* Logging
* Event System
* Configuration
* Threading / Job System
* Entity Component System (ECS)

```text
Core
├── GameLoop
├── Time
├── Memory
├── Events
├── Logger
├── Threads
└── ECS
```

### 2. Rendering Engine

Responsible for displaying graphics.

```text
Renderer
├── Graphics API
├── Camera
├── Mesh
├── Texture
├── Material
├── Shader
├── Lighting
├── Shadows
├── Post Processing
└── Particle System
```

Typical pipeline:

```text
Scene
  ↓
Culling
  ↓
Render Queue
  ↓
Shaders
  ↓
GPU
  ↓
Screen
```

### 3. Physics Engine

Handles physical simulation.

```text
Physics
├── Rigid Body
├── Collider
├── Collision Detection
├── Collision Resolution
├── Raycast
├── Gravity
├── Constraints
└── Physics World
```

### 4. Input System

Handles player interaction.

```text
Input
├── Keyboard
├── Mouse
├── Gamepad
├── Touch
└── Input Mapping
```

Example:

```text
W / ↑ / Gamepad Stick
          ↓
    MoveForward
          ↓
      Player
```

### 5. Audio Engine

```text
Audio
├── Audio Source
├── Audio Listener
├── Sound Effects
├── Music
├── Mixer
├── 3D Audio
└── Audio Streaming
```

### 6. Animation System

```text
Animation
├── Skeleton
├── Bones
├── Animation Clips
├── Animator
├── State Machine
├── Blend Tree
└── IK
```

Example:

```text
Idle → Walk → Run → Jump → Fall → Idle
```

### 7. Scene Management

Manages the game world.

```text
Scene
├── Entities
├── Components
├── Cameras
├── Lights
├── Environment
└── Systems
```

Example:

```text
Level 1
 ├── Player
 ├── Enemies
 ├── Buildings
 ├── Lights
 └── Camera
```

### 8. Asset Management

Loads and caches game resources.

```text
Asset Manager
├── Models
├── Textures
├── Shaders
├── Materials
├── Audio
├── Animations
└── Scenes
```

Important features:

* Asset loading
* Asset caching
* Resource lifetime management
* Hot reload
* Asset serialization

### 9. Scripting / Gameplay

Where game-specific logic lives.

```text
Gameplay
├── Player
├── Enemy
├── Weapons
├── Quests
├── Inventory
├── AI
└── Game Rules
```

### 10. AI System

```text
AI
├── Navigation
├── Pathfinding
├── Behavior Trees
├── State Machines
├── Decision Making
└── Perception
```

### 11. Networking

For multiplayer games.

```text
Networking
├── Client
├── Server
├── Replication
├── Serialization
├── Prediction
├── Interpolation
└── Synchronization
```

### 12. UI System

```text
UI
├── HUD
├── Menus
├── Buttons
├── Text
├── Inventory
├── Dialog
└── UI Layout
```

### 13. Scripting Language

You can expose engine functionality to another language:

```text
C++ Engine
     │
     ├── C++ Gameplay
     ├── Lua
     ├── C#
     └── Python
```

For a first engine, **C++ + embedded Lua** can be a useful architecture.

### 14. Editor

A professional engine usually needs development tools.

```text
Editor
├── Scene Editor
├── Inspector
├── Hierarchy
├── Asset Browser
├── Material Editor
├── Animation Editor
├── Console
├── Profiler
└── Debugger
```

### 15. Platform Layer

Abstracts the operating system and hardware.

```text
Platform
├── Windows
├── Linux
├── macOS
├── Android
└── iOS
```

It handles:

* Window creation
* File system
* Input
* Threads
* Timers
* GPU interfaces

---

## 🧩 Complete Component Map

```text
                         GAME ENGINE
                              │
       ┌──────────────────────┼──────────────────────┐
       │                      │                      │
     CORE                   RUNTIME                 TOOLS
       │                      │                      │
       ├── Game Loop          ├── Rendering          ├── Editor
       ├── ECS                ├── Physics            ├── Profiler
       ├── Memory             ├── Audio              ├── Debugger
       ├── Events             ├── Animation          ├── Asset Tools
       ├── Threads            ├── Input              └── Build Tools
       └── Logging            ├── AI
                              ├── Networking
                              ├── UI
                              ├── Scene
                              └── Scripting
                                       │
                              ┌────────▼────────┐
                              │ Platform Layer  │
                              ├─────────────────┤
                              │ OS / Window     │
                              │ File System     │
                              │ Input           │
                              │ Graphics API    │
                              └─────────────────┘
```

### If you're building your own engine

I'd implement the components in this order:

**Game Loop → Window/Input → Renderer → ECS → Asset Manager → Scene System → Physics → Audio → Animation → UI → Editor → Networking → Advanced Rendering.**

This gives you a working engine early instead of spending months building subsystems before you can actually run a game.
Absolutely. If your goal is to build a **serious game engine from scratch**, use a layered stack from **low-level systems → engine → tools → games**.

# 🎮 Game Engine — Full Technology Stack

```text
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    GAME / APPLICATION                    │
│       Gameplay • AI • UI • Scripts • Game Rules          │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                    ENGINE TOOLS                          │
│       Editor • Profiler • Debugger • Asset Pipeline      │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                    ENGINE RUNTIME                        │
│ Rendering • Physics • Audio • Animation • ECS • AI       │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                    ENGINE CORE                           │
│ Game Loop • Memory • Jobs • Events • Resources • Math    │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                    PLATFORM                              │
│ Window • Input • Filesystem • Threads • Networking       │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                 GRAPHICS / HARDWARE                      │
│ Vulkan • DirectX 12 • Metal • GPU • CPU                  │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                    OPERATING SYSTEM                      │
│ Windows • Linux • macOS                                  │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                       HARDWARE                           │
│ CPU • GPU • RAM • SSD • Audio • Controller               │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

## 1. Programming Languages

### Primary

**C++20/23**

Use C++ for:

* Engine core
* ECS
* Renderer
* Physics integration
* Memory management
* Multithreading
* Asset system
* Platform abstraction

```text
C++ 
 ├── Engine
 ├── Renderer
 ├── Physics
 ├── Audio
 ├── ECS
 └── Editor
```

### Secondary

**Lua**

For lightweight gameplay scripting.

```lua
player:set_position(10, 5, 2)
player:play_animation("run")
```

### Optional

**Python**

Use primarily for:

* Asset processing
* Build scripts
* Automation
* Data conversion
* ML/AI tooling

Don't make Python the core runtime language.

