# 8. ECS

You have two approaches.

### Build yourself

```text
Entity
Component
System
Registry
Archetype
Query
```

### Or use an existing ECS

Examples include:

* EnTT
* Flecs

A production architecture might eventually look like:

```text
World
 │
 ├── Entity
 │
 ├── Components
 │
 └── Systems
       ├── RenderSystem
       ├── PhysicsSystem
       ├── AISystem
       └── AnimationSystem
```

---

# 9. Memory Management

For a serious engine, don't rely entirely on general-purpose allocations.

Learn:

```text
malloc/free
new/delete
RAII
Smart Pointers
Memory Pools
Arena Allocators
Stack Allocators
Linear Allocators
Object Pools
```

Architecture:

```text
MemoryManager
├── FrameAllocator
├── LinearAllocator
├── PoolAllocator
└── GeneralAllocator
```

This becomes important for performance.

---

# 10. Multithreading / Job System

Modern games use many CPU cores.

Use:

```text
std::thread
std::jthread
std::atomic
std::mutex
std::condition_variable
```

Build:

```text
JobSystem
     │
     ├── Worker 1
     ├── Worker 2
     ├── Worker 3
     ├── Worker 4
     └── Worker N
```

Later:

```text
Task Graph
    ↓
Dependency Graph
    ↓
Worker Threads
```

---

# 11. Physics

Don't initially write your own complete physics engine.

Use:

### Jolt Physics

or

### Bullet

Architecture:

```text
PhysicsSystem
     ↓
Physics World
     ↓
RigidBody
     ↓
Collider
     ↓
Collision Detection
```

Later you can build your own physics implementation to understand the internals.

---

# 12. Audio

Use:

### miniaudio

Good for a lightweight engine.

Alternative:

### FMOD

For more advanced commercial audio workflows.

Architecture:

```text
AudioSystem
├── AudioSource
├── AudioListener
├── Mixer
├── SoundBank
└── 3D Audio
```

