## 🔥 Low-Level → High-Level Learning Path

```text
                    GAME
                     ↑
              Gameplay / Lua
                     ↑
          ┌──────────┴──────────┐
          │      ENGINE         │
          │                     │
          │ ECS  Physics Audio  │
          │ Rendering Animation │
          │ AI   Networking UI  │
          └──────────┬──────────┘
                     ↑
              ENGINE CORE
                     │
          ┌──────────┼──────────┐
          │          │          │
       Memory      Threads     Math
          │          │          │
          └──────────┼──────────┘
                     ↑
                 PLATFORM
                     │
              SDL3 / OS APIs
                     ↑
              GRAPHICS APIs
                     │
          Vulkan / DX12 / Metal
                     ↑
                  GPU/CPU
                     ↑
                HARDWARE
```

### 🚀 Build order

```text
1. C++23
   ↓
2. CMake + Git
   ↓
3. SDL3 Window
   ↓
4. Game Loop
   ↓
5. Input
   ↓
6. Math
   ↓
7. OpenGL Renderer
   ↓
8. ECS
   ↓
9. Asset Manager
   ↓
10. Scene System
   ↓
11. Vulkan Renderer
   ↓
12. Physics
   ↓
13. Audio
   ↓
14. Animation
   ↓
15. AI
   ↓
16. Networking
   ↓
17. Editor
   ↓
18. Profiler
   ↓
19. Scripting
   ↓
20. Complete Game
```

**Key principle:** don't try to build Vulkan, physics, audio, networking, ECS, and an editor simultaneously. Build a tiny **2D/3D vertical slice first**, then replace individual subsystems with more advanced implementations. This lets you turn the project into a real engine rather than a collection of unfinished modules.


Yes. If you want to **actually build your own game engine**, don't start with the editor, physics, AI, or networking. Build it bottom-up and make a small game run as early as possible.

# 🎮 Build Your Own Game Engine — Step by Step

## Overall roadmap

```text
PHASE 0   Development Environment
    ↓
PHASE 1   Window + Application
    ↓
PHASE 2   Game Loop + Time
    ↓
PHASE 3   Input System
    ↓
PHASE 4   Math Library
    ↓
PHASE 5   2D Renderer
    ↓
PHASE 6   Entity Component System
    ↓
PHASE 7   Asset System
    ↓
PHASE 8   Scene System
    ↓
PHASE 9   3D Renderer
    ↓
PHASE 10  Physics
    ↓
PHASE 11  Audio
    ↓
PHASE 12  Animation
    ↓
PHASE 13  AI
    ↓
PHASE 14  UI
    ↓
PHASE 15  Scripting
    ↓
PHASE 16  Editor
    ↓
PHASE 17  Profiler / Debugging
    ↓
PHASE 18  Networking
    ↓
PHASE 19  Packaging / Deployment
    ↓
       GAME ENGINE
```

---

# PHASE 0 — Development Environment

Start with:

```text
C++23
CMake
Ninja
Git
GitHub
VS Code / Visual Studio / CLion
```

Install:

```text
Compiler
├── Clang
└── MSVC/GCC

Build
├── CMake
└── Ninja

Debug
├── GDB/LLDB
├── RenderDoc
└── Tracy
```

Create:

```text
MyEngine/
├── Engine/
├── Game/
├── Editor/
├── Tests/
├── Assets/
├── Examples/
├── CMakeLists.txt
└── README.md
```

### First milestone

You should be able to run:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build
cmake --build build
```

and get:

```text
Hello MyEngine
```

---

# PHASE 1 — Create a Window

Use **SDL3** initially.

Architecture:

```text
Application
     ↓
Platform
     ↓
SDL3
     ↓
Operating System
```

Create:

```cpp
class Application
{
public:
    bool initialize();
    void run();
    void shutdown();
};
```

First target:

```text
┌──────────────────────────┐
│                          │
│      My Game Engine      │
│                          │
└──────────────────────────┘
```

### Learn

* Window creation
* Event handling
* Application lifecycle
* OS events

---

# PHASE 2 — Build the Game Loop

This is the heart of your engine.

```cpp
while (running)
{
    processEvents();

    float dt = timer.deltaTime();

    update(dt);

    render();
}
```

Architecture:

```text
Application
     │
     ▼
Game Loop
 ┌───┼────┐
 ▼   ▼    ▼
Input Update Render
```

Implement:

```text
Time
Clock
DeltaTime
FPS
FixedDeltaTime
```

Eventually:

```text
Variable Update
        +
Fixed Physics Update
```

Example:

```text
Frame
 │
 ├── Input
 ├── Update
 ├── Physics
 ├── Animation
 ├── Render
 └── Present
```

---

# PHASE 3 — Input System

Don't let gameplay depend directly on SDL.

Bad:

```cpp
SDL_GetKeyboardState(...)
```

inside your Player class.

Instead:

```text
SDL3
 ↓
Platform Input
 ↓
InputManager
 ↓
Gameplay
```

Create:

```cpp
input.isKeyDown(Key::W);
input.isMouseButtonDown(MouseButton::Left);
input.getMousePosition();
```

Then introduce actions:

```text
MoveForward
MoveBackward
Jump
Shoot
Interact
```

Mapping:

```text
W
↓
MoveForward
```

Later:

```text
Gamepad Stick
       ↓
MoveForward
```

---

# PHASE 4 — Math Library

Before serious graphics, learn/build the math layer.

```text
Vector2
Vector3
Vector4

Matrix3
Matrix4

Quaternion

Transform
```

Then:

```text
Ray
Plane
AABB
Sphere
Frustum
```

Important operations:

```text
Vector
├── Add
├── Subtract
├── Dot
├── Cross
├── Normalize
└── Length

Matrix
├── Translation
├── Rotation
├── Scale
├── Projection
└── View

Quaternion
├── Rotation
├── Multiply
└── Slerp
```

Initially use **GLM** rather than spending months writing your own math library.

---

# PHASE 5 — First Renderer

Start with **OpenGL** so you can understand the graphics pipeline without immediately dealing with Vulkan's complexity.

Architecture:

```text
Renderer
    ↓
Graphics API
    ↓
GPU
```

Build:

### Step 1

Clear screen.

```text
████████████████
████████████████
████████████████
```

### Step 2

Draw triangle.

```text
       ▲
      / \
     /   \
    /_____\
```

### Step 3

Draw rectangle.

### Step 4

Create vertex buffers.

```text
VertexBuffer
IndexBuffer
VertexArray
```

### Step 5

Shaders.

```text
Vertex Shader
      ↓
Rasterization
      ↓
Fragment Shader
```

### Step 6

Textures.

### Step 7

Camera.

### Step 8

Sprites.

At this point you can make a simple 2D game.

---

# PHASE 6 — ECS

Now introduce your entity system.

```text
Entity
  │
  ├── Transform
  ├── Sprite
  └── Player
```

Instead of giant inheritance hierarchies:

```text
GameObject
 ├── Player
 ├── Enemy
 ├── Vehicle
 └── NPC
```

use:

```text
Entity 100
├── Transform
├── Sprite
└── Health
```

Systems:

```text
MovementSystem
RenderSystem
PhysicsSystem
AudioSystem
```

For the first version, **EnTT** is a practical choice.

---

# PHASE 7 — Asset System

Now your engine needs to load resources.

```text
AssetManager
      │
 ┌────┼──────────┐
 ▼    ▼          ▼
Texture Mesh    Audio
```

Implement:

```cpp
TextureHandle loadTexture(path);
MeshHandle loadMesh(path);
ShaderHandle loadShader(path);
```

Add caching:

```text
load player.png
      ↓
Already loaded?
 ┌────┴─────┐
Yes         No
 ↓           ↓
Return      Load
             ↓
           Cache
```

Then implement asset handles instead of passing raw pointers everywhere.

---

# PHASE 8 — Scene System

Create:

```text
Scene
World
Entity
Component
```

Example:

```text
Scene: Level1

Entities:
 ├── Player
 ├── Enemy1
 ├── Enemy2
 ├── Camera
 └── Light
```

Save:

```text
Level1.scene
```

Load:

```text
SceneLoader
     ↓
Deserialize
     ↓
Create Entities
     ↓
Attach Components
```

Use JSON initially.

---

# PHASE 9 — Move to 3D

Now start the serious rendering work.

```text
2D Renderer
     ↓
3D Renderer
```

Learn:

```text
World Space
View Space
Clip Space
NDC
Perspective Projection
Depth Buffer
```

Build:

```text
Camera
   ↓
Mesh
   ↓
Material
   ↓
Shader
   ↓
Texture
   ↓
Lighting
```

Your milestone:

```text
        ☀
        │
      Cube
       /\
      /  \
     /____\
```

Then:

* Directional light
* Point light
* Spot light
* Shadows
* Normal mapping
* PBR

---

# PHASE 10 — Vulkan

Once you understand OpenGL, build a second renderer.

```text
Engine Renderer
       │
 ┌─────┴─────┐
 ▼           ▼
OpenGL      Vulkan
```

Create an abstraction:

```cpp
class Renderer
{
public:
    virtual void beginFrame() = 0;
    virtual void draw() = 0;
    virtual void endFrame() = 0;
};
```

Then:

```text
OpenGLRenderer
VulkanRenderer
```

Learn:

```text
Instance
Physical Device
Logical Device
Queue
Command Buffer
Swapchain
Render Pass
Pipeline
Descriptor Set
Synchronization
GPU Memory
```

Don't start here. Vulkan is much easier after you understand the graphics pipeline.

---

# PHASE 11 — Physics

Integrate **Jolt Physics** initially.

Your engine:

```text
PhysicsSystem
       ↓
Jolt
       ↓
Physics World
```

Components:

```text
RigidBody
Collider
CharacterController
```

Implement:

```text
Gravity
Collision
Raycast
Trigger
Rigid Body
```

Gameplay:

```text
Player
 ↓
Input
 ↓
Character Controller
 ↓
Physics
 ↓
Transform
 ↓
Renderer
```

